<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">398 CE – 410 CE · capital Guanggu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Murong Xianbei state, founded in Shandong by Murong De after the Northern Wei overran Later Yan, and destroyed by Liu Yu in 410.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a> capital <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhongshan">Zhongshan</a> in 397, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-de">Murong De</a>, a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, led followers south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-county">Huatai</a>, proclaiming himself King of Yan in 398. In 399 he moved east to Guanggu (near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qingzhou">Qingzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>) and in 400 declared himself emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Southern Yan ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> region with a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> military elite and Chinese officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-de">Murong De's</a> nephew and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chao">Murong Chao</a> alienated the court and raided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a>, seizing captives to train as court musicians.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The raids provoked the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a>, who invaded in 409, besieged Guanggu and took it in 410. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chao">Murong Chao</a> was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and thousands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> nobles were killed, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> states.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-yan.jpg" alt="Southern Yan" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_400_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>398 CE – 410 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guanggu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murong De, Murong Chao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan (Murong De)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=404&amp;focus=southern-yan" aria-label="Southern Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7437 1010 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8033 1602h0M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7757 1266h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7757 1266h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=404&amp;focus=southern-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
